{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788419592186,"sessionID":"ses_f99e39b61ffeVhyL6wKpvNfEvS","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"call_c9b15afcaf2f4246a4d342b9","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/testbed/docs/settings_files.md","newString":"## Layered environments on files\n\nIt is also possible to make dynaconf read the files separated by layered\nenvironments so each section or first level key is loaded as a\ndistinct environment.\n\n=== \"config.py\"\n    ```py\n    settings = Dynaconf(environments=True)\n    ```\n\n=== \"settings.toml\"\n    ```toml\n    [default]\n    name = \"\"\n    [development]\n    name = \"developer\"\n    [production]\n    name = \"admin\"\n    ```\n\n=== \"settings.yaml\"\n    ```yaml\n    default:\n        name: ''\n    development:\n        name: developer\n    production:\n        name: admin\n    ```\n\n=== \"settings.json\"\n    ```json\n    {\n        \"default\": {\n            \"name\": \"\"\n        },\n        \"development\": {\n            \"name\": \"developer\"\n        },\n        \"production\": {\n            \"name\": \"admin\"\n        }\n    }\n    ```\n\n=== \"settings.ini\"\n    ```ini\n    [default]\n    name = \"\"\n    [development]\n    name = \"developer\"\n    [production]\n    name = \"admin\"\n    ```\n\n!!! info\n    You can define a custom environment using the name you want, like `[testing]` or `[anything]`\n\n    `[default]` and `[global]` are the only environments that are special.\n\n=== \"program.py\"\n\n    Then in your program, you can use environment variables\n    to switch environments.\n\n    `#!bash export ENV_FOR_DYNACONF=development`\n\n    ```python\n    settings.name == \"developer\"\n    ```\n\n    `#!bash export ENV_FOR_DYNACONF=production`\n\n    ```python\n    settings.name == \"admin\"\n    ```\n\n!!! warning\n    On **Flask** and **Django** extensions the default behaviour is already\n    the layered environments.\n    Also to switch the environment you use `#!bash export FLASK_ENV=production` or `#!bash export DJANGO_ENV=production` respectively.\n\n!!! tip\n    It is also possible to switch environments programmatically passing\n    `env=\"development\"` to `Dynaconf` class on instantiation.\n\n### Bypassing environment processing with `load_file(env=False)`\n\nIf you want to load a configuration file without any environment-based filtering\nor layering, you can use the `env=False` parameter when calling `load_file()`.\nThis loads all top-level variables from the file directly, regardless of the\n`ENVIRONMENTS_FOR_DYNACONF` setting.\n\n**Available since version 3.2.11.**\n\n```python\nsettings = Dynaconf(environments=True)\nsettings.load_file(\"config.toml\", env=False)  # Loads all variables, ignores sections\n```\n\nThis is useful when:\n- You want to load a file with flat key-value pairs without any env sections\n- You have files that don't use the standard `[environment]` section format\n- You want consistent behavior regardless of your global environment settings","oldString":"## Layered environments on files\n\nIt is also possible to make dynaconf read the files separated by layered\nenvironments so each section or first level key is loaded as a\ndistinct environment.\n\n=== \"config.py\"\n    ```py\n    settings = Dynaconf(environments=True)\n    ```\n\n=== \"settings.toml\"\n    ```toml\n    [default]\n    name = \"\"\n    [development]\n    name = \"developer\"\n    [production]\n    name = \"admin\"\n    ```\n\n=== \"settings.yaml\"\n    ```yaml\n    default:\n        name: ''\n    development:\n        name: developer\n    production:\n        name: admin\n    ```\n\n=== \"settings.json\"\n    ```json\n    {\n        \"default\": {\n            \"name\": \"\"\n        },\n        \"development\": {\n            \"name\": \"developer\"\n        },\n        \"production\": {\n            \"name\": \"admin\"\n        }\n    }\n    ```\n\n=== \"settings.ini\"\n    ```ini\n    [default]\n    name = \"\"\n    [development]\n    name = \"developer\"\n    [production]\n    name = \"admin\"\n    ```\n\n!!! info\n    You can define a custom environment using the name you want, like `[testing]` or `[anything]`\n\n    `[default]` and `[global]` are the only environments that are special.\n\n=== \"program.py\"\n\n    Then in your program, you can use environment variables\n    to switch environments.\n\n    `#!bash export ENV_FOR_DYNACONF=development`\n\n    ```python\n    settings.name == \"developer\"\n    ```\n\n    `#!bash export ENV_FOR_DYNACONF=production`\n\n    ```python\n    settings.name == \"admin\"\n    ```\n\n!!! warning\n    On **Flask** and **Django** extensions the default behaviour is already\n    the layered environments.\n    Also to switch the environment you use `#!bash export FLASK_ENV=production` or `#!bash export DJANGO_ENV=production` respectively.\n\n!!! tip\n    It is also possible to switch environments programmatically passing\n    `env=\"development\"` to `Dynaconf` class on instantiation."},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /testbed/docs/settings_files.md\n===================================================================\n--- /testbed/docs/settings_files.md\n+++ /testbed/docs/settings_files.md\n@@ -215,8 +215,27 @@\n !!! tip\n     It is also possible to switch environments programmatically passing\n     `env=\"development\"` to `Dynaconf` class on instantiation.\n \n+### Bypassing environment processing with `load_file(env=False)`\n+\n+If you want to load a configuration file without any environment-based filtering\n+or layering, you can use the `env=False` parameter when calling `load_file()`.\n+This loads all top-level variables from the file directly, regardless of the\n+`ENVIRONMENTS_FOR_DYNACONF` setting.\n+\n+**Available since version 3.2.11.**\n+\n+```python\n+settings = Dynaconf(environments=True)\n+settings.load_file(\"config.toml\", env=False)  # Loads all variables, ignores sections\n+```\n+\n+This is useful when:\n+- You want to load a file with flat key-value pairs without any env sections\n+- You have files that don't use the standard `[environment]` section format\n+- You want consistent behavior regardless of your global environment settings\n+\n ### YAML Caveats\n \n #### Nonetypes\n \n","filediff":{"file":"/testbed/docs/settings_files.md","patch":"Index: /testbed/docs/settings_files.md\n===================================================================\n--- /testbed/docs/settings_files.md\n+++ /testbed/docs/settings_files.md\n@@ -215,8 +215,27 @@\n !!! tip\n     It is also possible to switch environments programmatically passing\n     `env=\"development\"` to `Dynaconf` class on instantiation.\n \n+### Bypassing environment processing with `load_file(env=False)`\n+\n+If you want to load a configuration file without any environment-based filtering\n+or layering, you can use the `env=False` parameter when calling `load_file()`.\n+This loads all top-level variables from the file directly, regardless of the\n+`ENVIRONMENTS_FOR_DYNACONF` setting.\n+\n+**Available since version 3.2.11.**\n+\n+```python\n+settings = Dynaconf(environments=True)\n+settings.load_file(\"config.toml\", env=False)  # Loads all variables, ignores sections\n+```\n+\n+This is useful when:\n+- You want to load a file with flat key-value pairs without any env sections\n+- You have files that don't use the standard `[environment]` section format\n+- You want consistent behavior regardless of your global environment settings\n+\n ### YAML Caveats\n \n #### Nonetypes\n \n","additions":19,"deletions":0},"truncated":false},"title":"docs/settings_files.md","time":{"start":1788419592169,"end":1788419592181}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_0661d4aaf001E1wGHcWY5LR2xQ","sessionID":"ses_f99e39b61ffeVhyL6wKpvNfEvS","messageID":"msg_0661d44c7001QUiveypcWJWZqv"}}
