{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788419641388,"sessionID":"ses_f99e39b61ffeVhyL6wKpvNfEvS","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"call_a8a1dd9903af4542bb91fdd4","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/testbed/docs/settings_files.md","newString":"### Bypassing environment processing with `load_file(env=False)`\n\nIf you want to load a configuration file without any environment-based filtering\nor layering, you can use the `env=False` parameter when calling `load_file()`.\nThis loads all sections from the file directly, regardless of the\n`ENVIRONMENTS_FOR_DYNACONF` setting and which environment is currently active.\n\n**Available since version 3.2.11.**\n\n```python\nsettings = Dynaconf(environments=True)\nsettings.load_file(\"config.toml\", env=False)\n```\n\nWhen `env=False` is used:\n- All sections from the file (e.g., `[default]`, `[development]`, `[production]`)\n  are loaded as nested dictionaries\n- Top-level variables (outside any section) are accessible directly\n- The current environment setting is ignored\n\nExample:\n\n```toml\n# config.toml\n[default]\napi_url = \"default_url\"\n\n[development]\napi_url = \"dev_url\"\ndebug = true\n\n[production]\napi_url = \"prod_url\"\ndebug = false\n```\n\n```python\nsettings = Dynaconf(environments=True, ENV_FOR_DYNACONF=\"production\")\n\n# Without env=False: only production section is loaded\nsettings.load_file(\"config.toml\")\nsettings.api_url  # \"prod_url\"\n\n# With env=False: all sections are loaded\nsettings.load_file(\"config.toml\", env=False)\nsettings.PRODUCTION.api_url  # \"prod_url\"\nsettings.DEVELOPMENT.api_url  # \"dev_url\"\nsettings.api_url  # None (top-level access doesn't work for section keys)\n```\n\nThis is useful when:\n- You want to load a file with multiple environment sections\n- You have files that don't use the standard `[environment]` section format\n- You want consistent behavior regardless of your global environment settings","oldString":"### Bypassing environment processing with `load_file(env=False)`\n\nIf you want to load a configuration file without any environment-based filtering\nor layering, you can use the `env=False` parameter when calling `load_file()`.\nThis loads all top-level variables from the file directly, regardless of the\n`ENVIRONMENTS_FOR_DYNACONF` setting.\n\n**Available since version 3.2.11.**\n\n```python\nsettings = Dynaconf(environments=True)\nsettings.load_file(\"config.toml\", env=False)  # Loads all variables, ignores sections\n```\n\nThis is useful when:\n- You want to load a file with flat key-value pairs without any env sections\n- You have files that don't use the standard `[environment]` section format\n- You want consistent behavior regardless of your global environment settings"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /testbed/docs/settings_files.md\n===================================================================\n--- /testbed/docs/settings_files.md\n+++ /testbed/docs/settings_files.md\n@@ -219,20 +219,56 @@\n ### Bypassing environment processing with `load_file(env=False)`\n \n If you want to load a configuration file without any environment-based filtering\n or layering, you can use the `env=False` parameter when calling `load_file()`.\n-This loads all top-level variables from the file directly, regardless of the\n-`ENVIRONMENTS_FOR_DYNACONF` setting.\n+This loads all sections from the file directly, regardless of the\n+`ENVIRONMENTS_FOR_DYNACONF` setting and which environment is currently active.\n \n **Available since version 3.2.11.**\n \n ```python\n settings = Dynaconf(environments=True)\n-settings.load_file(\"config.toml\", env=False)  # Loads all variables, ignores sections\n+settings.load_file(\"config.toml\", env=False)\n ```\n \n+When `env=False` is used:\n+- All sections from the file (e.g., `[default]`, `[development]`, `[production]`)\n+  are loaded as nested dictionaries\n+- Top-level variables (outside any section) are accessible directly\n+- The current environment setting is ignored\n+\n+Example:\n+\n+```toml\n+# config.toml\n+[default]\n+api_url = \"default_url\"\n+\n+[development]\n+api_url = \"dev_url\"\n+debug = true\n+\n+[production]\n+api_url = \"prod_url\"\n+debug = false\n+```\n+\n+```python\n+settings = Dynaconf(environments=True, ENV_FOR_DYNACONF=\"production\")\n+\n+# Without env=False: only production section is loaded\n+settings.load_file(\"config.toml\")\n+settings.api_url  # \"prod_url\"\n+\n+# With env=False: all sections are loaded\n+settings.load_file(\"config.toml\", env=False)\n+settings.PRODUCTION.api_url  # \"prod_url\"\n+settings.DEVELOPMENT.api_url  # \"dev_url\"\n+settings.api_url  # None (top-level access doesn't work for section keys)\n+```\n+\n This is useful when:\n-- You want to load a file with flat key-value pairs without any env sections\n+- You want to load a file with multiple environment sections\n - You have files that don't use the standard `[environment]` section format\n - You want consistent behavior regardless of your global environment settings\n \n ### YAML Caveats\n","filediff":{"file":"/testbed/docs/settings_files.md","patch":"Index: /testbed/docs/settings_files.md\n===================================================================\n--- /testbed/docs/settings_files.md\n+++ /testbed/docs/settings_files.md\n@@ -219,20 +219,56 @@\n ### Bypassing environment processing with `load_file(env=False)`\n \n If you want to load a configuration file without any environment-based filtering\n or layering, you can use the `env=False` parameter when calling `load_file()`.\n-This loads all top-level variables from the file directly, regardless of the\n-`ENVIRONMENTS_FOR_DYNACONF` setting.\n+This loads all sections from the file directly, regardless of the\n+`ENVIRONMENTS_FOR_DYNACONF` setting and which environment is currently active.\n \n **Available since version 3.2.11.**\n \n ```python\n settings = Dynaconf(environments=True)\n-settings.load_file(\"config.toml\", env=False)  # Loads all variables, ignores sections\n+settings.load_file(\"config.toml\", env=False)\n ```\n \n+When `env=False` is used:\n+- All sections from the file (e.g., `[default]`, `[development]`, `[production]`)\n+  are loaded as nested dictionaries\n+- Top-level variables (outside any section) are accessible directly\n+- The current environment setting is ignored\n+\n+Example:\n+\n+```toml\n+# config.toml\n+[default]\n+api_url = \"default_url\"\n+\n+[development]\n+api_url = \"dev_url\"\n+debug = true\n+\n+[production]\n+api_url = \"prod_url\"\n+debug = false\n+```\n+\n+```python\n+settings = Dynaconf(environments=True, ENV_FOR_DYNACONF=\"production\")\n+\n+# Without env=False: only production section is loaded\n+settings.load_file(\"config.toml\")\n+settings.api_url  # \"prod_url\"\n+\n+# With env=False: all sections are loaded\n+settings.load_file(\"config.toml\", env=False)\n+settings.PRODUCTION.api_url  # \"prod_url\"\n+settings.DEVELOPMENT.api_url  # \"dev_url\"\n+settings.api_url  # None (top-level access doesn't work for section keys)\n+```\n+\n This is useful when:\n-- You want to load a file with flat key-value pairs without any env sections\n+- You want to load a file with multiple environment sections\n - You have files that don't use the standard `[environment]` section format\n - You want consistent behavior regardless of your global environment settings\n \n ### YAML Caveats\n","additions":40,"deletions":4},"truncated":false},"title":"docs/settings_files.md","time":{"start":1788419641371,"end":1788419641383}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_0661e0c6c001Sm6bxw0gUvk0EL","sessionID":"ses_f99e39b61ffeVhyL6wKpvNfEvS","messageID":"msg_0661e080700179Lx782wLvmsU3"}}
